// Provokes the one warning the compiler raises, so [ERR-010] can be checked
// against what is actually printed.
//
// ⚠️ This is not a probe and not a conformance case.  It is the SOURCE the
// [ERR-010] warning line is compared with, run live by spec/spec.sh -- the same
// treatment the keyword table, Annex B and the [COL-003] matrix get.  A message
// quoted in a specification and checked by nobody is exactly the rot those
// three checks exist to prevent.
//
// ⚠️ THE LINE NUMBER IS PART OF WHAT IS CHECKED.  Nothing above the call may
// move without [ERR-010]'s example moving with it.

procedure Log (Level : String);                   begin end
procedure Log (Level : String, Message : String); begin end
procedure Log (Code : Integer);                   begin end

Log ('warn');

// Two arguments, and only one signature takes two.  The count decides the call,
// nothing is left to select at run time, and nothing is warned about -- which is
// checked as surely as the line above, because spec.sh compares EVERY [WARN]
// line this file raises against the one [ERR-010] states.
Log ('warn', 'disk');

// The second warning: a member of the name hides a visible extension, so the
// member takes the call [EXP-022] and the extension is not reached.  A class
// declaring a member in some later version is the one way an extension can be
// taken away silently, which is why the cost is reported where it is incurred.
//
// ⚠️ NOTHING ABOVE THIS MAY MOVE either -- the second [WARN] line [ERR-010]
// states carries its line number as surely as the first.
extension function Size (L : List) : Integer; begin Exit L.Length; end

class Box;
begin
    function Size () : Integer; begin Exit 7; end
end

var B : Box := Box ();

WriteLn (B.Size ());

// The extension IS reached here, and nothing is warned about: a List has no
// member of that name.
WriteLn ([1, 2].Size ());

// The third warning: 'and' tests truthiness and yields an OPERAND [EXP-010]
// where '&' combines bits [EXP-023].  A reader arriving from Pascal -- where
// 'and' on integers is bitwise -- writes the first and means the second, and
// gets a plausible wrong number: this prints 3 where '&' would give 2.
//
// ⚠️ NOTHING ABOVE THIS MAY MOVE either.  The third [WARN] line [ERR-010]
// states carries its line number as surely as the first two.
var Mask : Integer := 6;
var Bits : Integer := 3;

WriteLn (Mask and Bits);
